program            = 001_character_escapes
area               = 11_literals_and_strings
description        = Character escape sequences: simple, octal and hexadecimal forms, read through unsigned char, folded and volatile-runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Free of undefined and unspecified behaviour by construction:
- No signed integer overflow. Every printed value originates from an unsigned char
  element (range 0..255) widened to int, or from a character constant whose value is
  at most 127. All loop counters are int and bounded by a small array size.
- No shift operations of any kind, so no shift count can be out of range.
- No out-of-bounds access. Each loop runs from 0 to (sizeof array - 1) exclusive,
  which visits exactly the non-null elements. The three runtime accesses index
  (sizeof array - 2), the last non-null element. No one-past-end pointer is formed
  and nothing beyond an array is read.
- No aliasing violation. Each array is declared as unsigned char and is read only
  through that declared type.
- No uninitialized read. Every array has a static const initializer and idx_zero has
  a static initializer.
- No string literal is modified. The arrays are distinct objects initialized from
  string literals as C11 6.7.9p14 permits, and each is additionally const-qualified.
- No object is modified twice between sequence points; no argument to any call has a
  side effect; nothing depends on padding bytes or on the addresses of unrelated
  objects; no address or pointer value is printed.
- Verified empirically: the default strict warning gate (-Wall -Wextra -pedantic
  -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror) produces zero diagnostics, and
  -fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all runs to completion with no
  output and exit status 0.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Plain char is never used to hold or print an escape value. Every character datum is
read through unsigned char, so the measured plain-char signedness difference (plain
char is signed on x86_64 and i686 but unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64) cannot be
observed in this program's output. Handled by construction, not by a marker.

High-valued escapes (\200, \376, \377, \x80, \xfe, \xff) appear only inside string
literals that initialize static const unsigned char arrays, and are then indexed.
They are never written as plain character constants, because a plain character
constant of value 255 evaluates to -1 on the signed-char targets and to 255 on the
unsigned-char targets. Character constants are used only for values at most 127,
where every target agrees.

Only int-typed values are printed, always with %d. No long, no size_t and no pointer
is printed, so the measured i686 width difference (sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *)
are 4 on i686 and 8 on the other three targets) has no effect on the output.

Escape decoding, octal formatting and hexadecimal formatting were measured
byte-identical across all four targets at every optimization level, so this program
is fully cross-comparable and all three oracles are enabled.
END
